  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],$\nfind $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi D}{6}).$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi D}{6})=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |",
  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries ${\\lambda }_{1},\u2026,{\\lambda }_{n},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(D)=[\\begin{aligned}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & \u22ef & 0 \\\\ \u22ee & \u22f1 & \u22ee \\\\ 0 & \u22ef & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi D}{6}) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{6}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
